WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.577
Prompts and outputs should be treated as potentially sensitive data.

00:00:04.577 --> 00:00:15.412
They may contain user identifiers, private documents, source code, secrets, business plans, regulated content, customer records, or internal operational details.

00:00:15.412 --> 00:00:25.642
Even if the model service is private, the surrounding logs, analytics, debug traces, prompt history, and storage systems can become data exposure paths.

00:00:25.642 --> 00:00:28.872
Log minimization is a practical privacy control.

00:00:28.872 --> 00:00:42.804
Capture enough information to operate the service, investigate errors, and support audit requirements, but avoid storing full prompts and outputs unless there is a clear need and an approved retention model.

00:00:42.804 --> 00:00:46.977
Redaction can help at a high level, but it is not a guarantee.

00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:54.380
Sensitive values may appear in unexpected forms, and overly broad debug logging can defeat careful API design.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:01.850
Retention policies should define what is stored, where it is stored, who can access it, and when it is deleted.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:09.321
Prompt history may be valuable for user experience or troubleshooting, but it also creates privacy obligations.

00:01:09.321 --> 00:01:15.243
Local storage, cloud storage, backups, and analytics pipelines should all be considered.

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:23.656
Data copied into secondary systems can outlive the original request unless deletion and retention are designed intentionally.

00:01:23.656 --> 00:01:28.771
Privacy expectations should be communicated to application owners and users.

00:01:28.771 --> 00:01:30.790
If prompts are logged, say so.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:35.030
If outputs are retained for review, define who can review them.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:41.087
If data is sent to a hosted provider, understand provider terms and organizational policy.

00:01:41.087 --> 00:01:45.664
A well-run LLM API does not rely on vague assumptions about privacy.

00:01:45.664 --> 00:01:49.500
It defines data handling as part of the service contract.
